             "lines": ["type Counter struct{ N int }", "", "func (c Counter) IncValue() {", "    c.N++", "}", "", "func (c *Counter) IncPointer() {", "    c.N++", "}"], "highlight": [2, 6]},
   "face": "thinking", "palette": "blue",
   "say": "Go Part 22, pointer versus value receivers. These two methods have the exact same body. So why does only one of them really work?"},
  {"title": [{"text": "Let's run it", "box": "yellow"}, {"text": "0, then 1", "color": "yellow"}],
   "image": {"type": "code", "file": "main.go",
             "lines": ["c := Counter{N: 0}", "c.IncValue()", "fmt.Println(c.N)", "c.IncPointer()", "fmt.Println(c.N)"],
             "highlight": [1], "output": ["0", "1"]},
   "face": "worried", "palette": "orange", "reveal": [0.4, 0.5, 0.5, 0.5, 0.5, 1.2],
   "say": "We call IncValue, and N is still zero. Then we call IncPointer, and N becomes one."},
  {"title": [{"text": "Value receiver", "box": "yellow"}, {"text": "gets a copy", "box": "red"}],
   "image": {"type": "refs", "names": ["c", "copy"], "boxes": [["0"], ["1"]],
             "links": [[0, 0], [1, 1]], "new": [[1, 0]], "label": "TWO COPIES OF N"},
   "face": "thinking", "palette": "purple",
   "say": "A value receiver gets a copy of the struct. The method adds one to the copy, and the copy is thrown away. Your c never changes."},
  {"title": [{"text": "Pointer", "box": "yellow"}, {"text": "receiver"}, {"text": "= the real one", "box": "red"}],
   "image": {"type": "refs", "names": ["c", "*c"], "boxes": [["1"]],
             "links": [[0, 0], [1, 0]], "new": [[0, 0]], "label": "ONE N IN MEMORY"},
   "face": "happy", "palette": "blue",
   "say": "A pointer receiver gets the address of c. So it changes the real counter. Go even takes the address for you when you write c dot IncPointer."},
